{"name":"White Whale","hex":"#edeeef","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white whale","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of beluga (“the beluga whale, Delphinapterus leucas, a cetacean found in the Arctic Ocean”)","Any whale of the family Monodontidae, which comprises the beluga whales and narwhals (Monodon monoceros), and some extinct species."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_whale"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Whale (Brooklyn Nine-Nine)","blocks":[{"text":"\"White Whale\" is the 21st episode of the fifth season of the American television police sitcom series Brooklyn Nine-Nine, and the 111th overall episode of the series. The episode was written by Matt Lawton & Carol Kolb and directed by Matthew Nodella. It aired on Fox in the United States on May 13, 2018. The episode features guest appearances from Allison Tolman and Carol Herman."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Whale_(Brooklyn_Nine-Nine)"},{"title":"White Whale (band)","blocks":[{"text":"White Whale is an American indie rock band from Lawrence, Kansas, composed of former members of Butterglory, The Get Up Kids, and Thee Higher Burning Fire. They are signed to Merge Records and released their first full-length album WW1 on July 25, 2006."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Whale_(band)"},{"title":"Beluga whale","blocks":[{"text":"The beluga whale or the white whale is an Arctic and sub-Arctic cetacean. It is one of two living members of the family Monodontidae, along with the narwhal, and the only member of the genus Delphinapterus. It is also known as the sea canary, due to its high-pitched calls; and the melonhead, though that more commonly refers to the melon-headed whale, which is an oceanic dolphin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Oceanogr%C3%A0fic_29102004.jpg/330px-Oceanogr%C3%A0fic_29102004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beluga_whale"},{"title":"Whale","blocks":[{"text":"Whales are a widely distributed and diverse group of fully aquatic placental marine mammals. As an informal and colloquial grouping, they correspond to large members of the infraorder Cetacea, i.e. all cetaceans apart from dolphins and porpoises. Dolphins and porpoises may be considered whales from a formal, cladistic perspective. Whales, dolphins and porpoises belong to the order Cetartiodactyla, which consists of even-toed ungulates. Their closest non-cetacean living relatives are the hippopotamuses, from which they and other cetaceans diverged about 54 million years ago. The two parvorders of whales, baleen whales (Mysticeti) and toothed whales (Odontoceti), are thought to have had their last common ancestor around 34 million years ago. Mysticetes include four extant (living) families: Balaenopteridae, Balaenidae, Cetotheriidae, and Eschrichtiidae. Odontocetes include the Monodontidae, Physeteridae, Kogiidae, and Ziphiidae, as well as the six families of dolphins and porpoises which are not considered whales in the informal sense."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Southern_right_whale.jpg/330px-Southern_right_whale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Narwhal_x_Beluga.jpg/500px-Narwhal_x_Beluga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Narwhal_x_Beluga.jpg/1280px-Narwhal_x_Beluga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Narwhal_x_Beluga.jpg/500px-Narwhal_x_Beluga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANarwhal_x_Beluga.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Narwhal_hybridisation.webp/500px-Narwhal_hybridisation.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Narwhal_hybridisation.webp/500px-Narwhal_hybridisation.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANarwhal_hybridisation.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Squelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg/500px-Squelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Squelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg/1280px-Squelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Squelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg/500px-Squelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASquelette_Delphinapterus_leucas_rdl.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Delphinapterus_leucas_3d_scan_Natural_History_Museum_University_of_Pisa_C_277.stl/500px-Delphinapterus_leucas_3d_scan_Natural_History_Museum_University_of_Pisa_C_277.stl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Delphinapterus_leucas_3d_scan_Natural_History_Museum_University_of_Pisa_C_277.stl/1280px-Delphinapterus_leucas_3d_scan_Natural_History_Museum_University_of_Pisa_C_277.stl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Delphinapterus_leucas_3d_scan_Natural_History_Museum_University_of_Pi